--TEST--
Every `Core\Reflect\MethodInfo` row's `isPublic` agrees with whether `Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::call` runs that method, asserted as a count over the whole roster — `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`: the roster reads metadata and `call` acts, so the two
// are answered by different code — the bit comes down from the declaration and
// the refusal comes from the call path. This asks them the same question about
// every method at once and counts the agreements, so a roster that grew its own
// idea of visibility fails here while still printing plausibly row by row.
class Door {
    public function open(): string {
        return "open";
    }

    public function shut(): string {
        return "shut";
    }

    protected function frame(): string {
        return "frame";
    }

    private function hinge(): string {
        return "hinge";
    }
}

var $door = new Door();
var $info = Core\Reflect::forObject($door);

int $total = 0;
int $agree = 0;
int $ran = 0;
int $nullary = 0;
foreach ($info->methods() as Core\Reflect\MethodInfo $method) {
    $total = $total + 1;
    if ($method->parameterCount() == 0) {
        $nullary = $nullary + 1;
    }
    bool $called = false;
    try {
        var $answer = $info->call($door, $method->name(), []);
        $called = true;
    } catch (LogicError $refused) {
        $called = false;
    }
    if ($called) {
        $ran = $ran + 1;
    }
    if ($called == $method->isPublic()) {
        $agree = $agree + 1;
    }
}

// Four rows, four agreements, and two of them ran: the count of agreements
// alone would be met by a roster that called nothing and reported nothing
// public, so the number that ran is named beside it.
echo $total, " rows\n";
echo $agree, " agree\n";
echo $ran, " ran\n";

// Every empty argument list above was legitimate, which the roster is what
// says: `call` judges a list against the callee's own arity, so a row claiming
// a parameter this loop did not pass would have refused for the wrong reason
// and still counted as an agreement with a `private` bit.
echo $nullary, " nullary\n";

// And `hasMethod` answers for the whole roster the same way — including the
// two rows no call site outside the class may reach, which is what tells a
// closed method from one that was never declared.
echo $info->hasMethod("hinge") ? "declared" : "absent", "\n";
echo $info->hasMethod("frame") ? "declared" : "absent", "\n";
echo $info->hasMethod("hindge") ? "declared" : "absent", "\n";
--EXPECT--
4 rows
4 agree
2 ran
4 nullary
declared
declared
absent
